.TH QUANUXCTL-SPREADER-PACKAGE 1 "February 2026" "QuanuX 1.0.0" "QuanuX Commands"
.SH NAME
quanuxctl-spreader-package \- Transpile Spreader JSON into Cython C++ and compile an Execution Engine.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quanuxctl spreader package
.I <STRATEGY_JSON_PATH>
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B quanuxctl spreader package
forges Spreader formulas into strict C++ architectures. 

The command replaces arbitrary string templating with the "Cython Forge" pipeline. It evaluates the provided IR (Intermediate Representation) JSON and transpiles the math into two distinct artifacts:
1. `strategy_injected.hpp` (A strictly-typed `extern "C++"` header injected into the Spreader build tree).
2. `strategy_wrapper.pyx` (A Cython Python-module allowing the QuanuX Crucible Backtester to evaluate the precise C++ logic).

To guarantee execution integrity within the 59ns Engine Loop:
*   The command asserts that `StrategyState` compiles to <= 64 bytes to eliminate L1 cache spillage.
*   The C++ transpilation avoids all usage of PyObjects.
*   The execution concludes by generating a `.sig` Cryptographic Manifest derived from the SHA-256 hash of the output machine code for remote validation.
.SH DEPLOYMENT PIPELINE
After successful packaging, the CLI outputs a localized `.sig` manifest and prints the final binary hash (e.g., `quanux-spreader-063e07ea`).
Use `quanuxctl spreader deploy` to move this payload into production.
